        <reference target="https://www.iso.org/standard/37338.html" anchor="ISO24624">
          <front>
            <title>Language resource management</title>
            <author>
              <organization ascii="International Organization for Standardization" abbrev="ISO">International Organization for Standardization</organization>
            </author>
            <date month="August" year="2016"></date>
            <abstract>
              <t>ISO 24624:2016 specifies rules for representing transcriptions of audio- and video-recorded spoken interactions in XML documents based on the guidelines of the TEI. As a secondary objective, the document aims to relate transcribed data with standards for annotated corpora. It is applicable to transcription data for studies in sociolinguistics, conversation analysis, dialectology, corpus linguistics, corpus lexicography, language technology, qualitative social studies and other transcription data of recorded spoken language. It is not applicable to other forms of transcription, most importantly transcriptions of hand-written manuscripts.Annex A gives a fully encoded example and Annex B provides an element index and an attribute index.</t>
            </abstract>
          </front>
          <format target="https://www.iso.org/standard/37338.html" type="src"></format>
          <format target="https://www.iso.org/obp/ui/#!iso:std:37338:en" type="obp"></format>
          <format target="https://www.iso.org/contents/data/standard/03/73/37338.detail.rss" type="rss"></format>
          <refcontent>ISO 24624:2016</refcontent>
        </reference>
        <reference target="https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc2119" anchor="RFC2119">
          <front>
            <title>Key words for use in RFCs to Indicate Requirement Levels</title>
            <author fullname="S. Bradner" asciiFullname="S. Bradner"></author>
            <date month="March" year="1997"></date>
            <abstract>
              <t>In many standards track documents several words are used to signify the requirements in the specification.  These words are often capitalized. This document defines these words as they should be interpreted in IETF documents.  This document specifies an Internet Best Current Practices for the Internet Community, and requests discussion and suggestions for improvements.</t>
            </abstract>
          </front>
          <format target="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/relaton/relaton-data-ietf/master/data/reference.RFC.2119.xml" type="xml"></format>
